<body>
<h1>Unit 24: Nuclear Physics</h1><p class="meta">Physics XI  |  NEB (Grade 11)  |  Phy. 101 &nbsp;•&nbsp; Content area: Modern Physics</p><p class="summary">Nuclear physics is where conservation laws become non-negotiable and where a handful of nucleons produce an enormous energy scale. The binding-energy curve is the centre of the unit: it explains why fusion and fission both release energy, and only in different regimes.</p><h2>What the model asserts</h2><ol><li>The nuclear radius scales as R = R0 A^(1/3) with R0 about 1.2 fm, which means nuclear density is roughly constant at about 2.3e17 kg/m^3.</li><li>Binding energy per nucleon peaks near iron-56 at about 8.8 MeV per nucleon, so fusion up to iron and fission beyond it both release energy.</li><li>Einstein's E = mc^2 converts the mass defect into binding energy; 1 u of mass corresponds to 931.5 MeV.</li><li>Radioactive decay is statistical: the half-life is a property of the nucleus, and no individual atom decides when it decays.</li><li>Neutron decay and nuclear reactions conserve charge, baryon number, energy, momentum and angular momentum, and every proposed reaction must satisfy all of them.</li><li>Doubly magic nuclei such as oxygen-16, with closed proton and neutron shells, show binding energies well above the smooth trend of their neighbours.</li><li>Binding energy per nucleon climbs to iron-56 and falls beyond it, which is the whole reason fusion powers stars and fission powers reactors: both release energy by moving matter towards the peak.</li></ol><h2>Conditions, assumptions and limits</h2><ul><li>The semi-empirical mass formula is fitted to known nuclei, so its predictions are most reliable in the region where the coefficients were fitted and degrade for exotic nuclei.</li><li>Alpha decay proceeds by quantum tunnelling, and the extreme sensitivity of the half-life to the alpha energy and Q-value - the Geiger-Nuttall relation - is the essential experimental handle.</li><li>Fission and fusion release energy only because the products sit nearer the peak of the binding-energy curve than the fuel does.</li><li>Quasi-stable nuclei are predicted by the liquid-drop model, but shell effects require the nuclear shell model, and neither is exact.</li><li>The liquid-drop model gets nuclear masses roughly right but misses the shell and pairing effects that explain the magic numbers and the fine structure of fission barriers.</li></ul><h2>Exam coverage — 7 NEB topics</h2><ul><li>Nucleus: discovery of nucleus</li><li>Nuclear density; mass number; atomic number</li><li>Atomic mass; isotopes</li><li>Einstein's mass-energy relation</li><li>Mass defect, packing fraction, binding energy per nucleon</li><li>Creation and annihilation</li><li>Nuclear fission and fusion</li></ul><h2>Common misconceptions — 3 to unlearn</h2><div class="mystery"><p><b>“A heavier nucleus is always more tightly bound, so the most massive nuclei are the most stable.”</b></p><p><b>Why it fails:</b> Binding energy per nucleon is not monotonic in mass number. It rises to a maximum near iron-56 and then falls, which is why heavy nuclei are unstable and can release energy by fission.</p><p><b>Correct:</b> The binding energy per nucleon is about 8.8 MeV at iron-56, about 7.6 MeV for uranium-238 and about 1.1 MeV for a lead nucleus at the extreme end. Nuclei heavier than iron gain energy by splitting, and nuclei lighter than iron gain it by fusing, because in both cases the products lie closer to the maximum of the curve.</p></div><div class="mystery"><p><b>“A radioactive atom decays because it is unstable and 'spits out' radiation at a random moment.”</b></p><p><b>Why it fails:</b> The decay is not a random event in the sense of being unpredictable in principle: the probability per unit time is fixed by the nucleus, but the instant at which any particular nucleus decays is not determined in advance.</p><p><b>Correct:</b> Radioactive decay obeys the law dN/dt = -lambda N, with a half-life T = ln2 / lambda that is a property of the isotope, not of the individual atom. Over many nuclei the law is exact; for a single nucleus only the probability is known. This statistical character is the basis of radiometric dating.</p></div><div class="mystery"><p><b>“In nuclear reactions mass is not conserved, because mass can be converted into energy.”</b></p><p><b>Why it fails:</b> Mass is not lost; it is the total mass-energy that is conserved. The 'missing' mass in a reaction is exactly the mass equivalent of the released energy, and the rest masses of the products add to more than the rest mass of the reactants.</p><p><b>Correct:</b> In every reaction the total mass-energy, and therefore the total baryon number and the total charge, is conserved. For example, 4 neutrons converting into a helium nucleus release 0.006 u of mass, which is 5.6 MeV of energy, and the sum of the rest masses of the products is greater than the sum of the rest masses of the reactants by exactly that amount.</p></div><h2>Workbook — 3 questions with answers</h2><div class="item"><p class="status">numerical</p><p><b>Find the energy equivalent of 1 unified atomic mass unit, and the energy released in the fusion of four protons to form one helium-4 nucleus with the release of two positrons and two neutrinos (mass defect about 0.0187 u).</b></p><p><b>Answer:</b> 1 u = 1.66054 x 10^-27 kg, so E = m c^2 = 1.66054 x 10^-27 x (2.9979 x 10^8)^2 = 1.4924 x 10^-10 J. In electron volts, 1.4924 x 10^-10 / 1.602 x 10^-19 = 9.315 x 10^8 eV = 931.5 MeV. For the fusion reaction, the mass defect of 0.0187 u corresponds to 0.0187 x 931.5 = 17.4 MeV, which is the energy released per helium-4 nucleus produced. This is the basis of fusion in the Sun and in thermonuclear weapons.</p></div><div class="item"><p class="status">derivation</p><p><b>Derive the binding energy per nucleon of a nucleus from the masses of its constituent nucleons, and explain why the packing fraction is the useful measure of the energy released.</b></p><p><b>Answer:</b> The mass of a nucleus is less than the sum of the masses of its free nucleons by the mass defect, dm = Z m_p + (A - Z) m_n - m_nucleus. The missing mass is the mass of the energy that holds the nucleus together, so the binding energy is B = dm c^2, and the binding energy per nucleon is B / A. The packing fraction is f = dm / (Z m_p + (A - Z) m_n), the fraction of the mass that is converted into binding energy, so B = f A (sum of the free nucleon masses) c^2. For iron-56, f is about 0.0087 and B/A is about 8.8 MeV per nucleon, the maximum of the curve; for uranium-238, f is about 0.0071 and B/A about 7.6 MeV.</p></div><div class="item"><p class="status">reasoning</p><p><b>Explain, using the binding energy per nucleon curve, why both fission of a heavy nucleus and fusion of a light nucleus release energy, while the splitting of a mid-mass nucleus absorbs energy.</b></p><p><b>Answer:</b> The binding energy per nucleon is a measure of how tightly bound each nucleon is, and a reaction releases energy when the products have a greater total binding energy than the reactants. For uranium-238, B/A is about 7.6 MeV; the fission products around mass 95 have B/A of about 8.5 MeV, so the products are more tightly bound and the difference, about 200 MeV, is released. For four protons, B/A is effectively zero since free protons are not bound, while the helium-4 product has B/A of 28.3/4 = 7.1 MeV, so about 28 MeV is released. Near the maximum of the curve, at iron-56, splitting a nucleus moves the products to a lower B/A, so the process absorbs energy instead. This single curve explains why the iron group is the end point of stellar fusion and why both fission and fusion are viable energy sources.</p></div><h2>Key numbers</h2><table><tr><th>Quantity</th><th>Value</th></tr><tr><td>Energy equivalent of 1 u</td><td>931.5 MeV</td></tr><tr><td>Binding energy per nucleon at iron-56</td><td>about 8.8 MeV</td></tr><tr><td>Nuclear radius constant R0</td><td>about 1.2 fm</td></tr><tr><td>Half-life of carbon-14</td><td>about 5730 years</td></tr></table><h2>Open questions and mysteries</h2><div class="mystery"><p><b>What is the neutrino's mass?</b></p><p class="status">Open question</p><p>The US Department of Energy reported KATRIN's direct upper bound of 0.45 eV/c^2 in 2026; the exact value remains unknown.</p></div><div class="mystery"><p><b>How strong are the shell effects that make magic nuclei so stable?</b></p><p class="status">Under active scrutiny</p><p>Magic numbers are firmly established experimentally, but their quantitative origin in the nucleon-nucleon interaction remains a subtle many-body problem.</p></div><div class="mystery"><p><b>What is the internal structure of the neutron?</b></p><p class="status">Frontier</p><p>The neutron is not a point particle, and its charge and magnetic structure is being probed at accelerators and in precision atomic-physics experiments.</p></div><h2>Inquiry prompts</h2><ol><li>Compute the binding energy per nucleon of a given isotope from the mass defect and place it relative to iron on the binding-energy curve.</li><li>Show that the activity of a radioactive sample falls exponentially, and estimate an age from the measured isotopic ratios.</li><li>Explain why the alpha-decay half-life is so sensitive to a small change in nuclear charge.</li></ol><h2>Next steps on this platform</h2><ul><li><a href="/class-11-notes/physics/chapters/nuclear-physics">Read the chapter notes: Nuclear Physics</a></li><li><a href="/lab/ph-3d-quantum">Quantum 3D lab</a></li><li><a href="/lab/ph-th-modern">Modern physics theory lab</a></li></ul><h2>Further reading</h2><ul><li><a href="https://www.energy.gov/science/np/articles/katrin-narrows-down-range-neutrinos-mass">US DOE: KATRIN narrows the range of the neutrino mass</a></li><li><a href="https://openstax.org/books/university-physics-volume-3/pages/index">OpenStax: University Physics Volume 3</a></li><li><a href="https://openstax.org/books/university-physics-volume-3/pages/10-1-properties-of-nuclei">OpenStax: Properties of Nuclei</a></li><li><a href="https://openstax.org/books/university-physics-volume-3/pages/10-2-nuclear-binding-energy">OpenStax: Nuclear Binding Energy</a></li><li><a href="https://openstax.org/books/university-physics-volume-3/pages/10-3-radioactive-decay">OpenStax: Radioactive Decay</a></li></ul><p class="note">Physics XI — Academic Companion — a companion brief, not a replacement for the prescribed NEB text. The open questions describe live research frontiers; they are enrichment, not a claim that the established school-level model is invalid. Check the prescribed text and syllabus for examinable scope.</p>
</body>
